CH = dict(num=19, tag="Economics · Ch 3", theme="Money as a medium of exchange, banks, formal and informal credit, self-help groups",
 title="Money and Credit",
 intro="Money makes exchange easy, and credit (loans) lets people produce and consume more than they can afford today. This chapter looks at modern forms of money, how banks use deposits to lend, the difference between formal and informal credit, and how self-help groups help the poor.",
 big_questions=["What are the modern forms of money?", "How do banks work?", "What are the terms of credit and sources of credit?", "How do self-help groups help the poor?"],
 blocks=[
 ("h2", "Money as a Medium of Exchange"),
 ("ul", ["In a **barter system**, goods are exchanged directly for other goods; it needs a **double coincidence of wants**: what one person wants to sell is what the other wants to buy. Money removes this difficulty because it is accepted as a **medium of exchange**; the use of money makes it easier to exchange.", "**Modern forms of money:** **currency** (paper notes and coins) and **deposits with banks**. Earlier, grains and cattle were used; today, coins and notes are used. In India the **Reserve Bank of India (RBI)** issues currency notes on behalf of the central government; **no one in India can refuse a payment made in rupees** (legal tender); it is illegal for anyone else to issue them.", "**Deposits with banks:** People deposit extra cash with banks and can withdraw it when needed. Because it can be withdrawn on demand, such deposits are called **demand deposits**. Payment can be made through **cheques** (instructions to the bank to pay a specific amount), so deposits also act as money; cheque payment has the added advantage of less need for cash. Today the use of debit cards, UPI and online transfers is widespread."]),
 ("note", "NOTE", "Money in the form of currency and demand deposits is accepted as a medium of payment, since it is **legally approved by the government**; deposits work as money because they are accepted widely, even though they are not legal tender."),
 ("h2", "Loan Activities of Banks"),
 ("ul", ["Banks keep only about **15 per cent** of deposits as cash with them, as only a small fraction of depositors come to withdraw money on any given day. They use the major portion of deposits to extend **loans**, which meet the credit needs of various people.", "Banks charge a **higher interest on loans than they pay on deposits**; the difference between these rates is their main source of income."]),
 ("h2", "Two Different Credit Situations"),
 ("note", "DEFINITION", "**Credit (loan)** is an agreement in which the lender supplies the borrower with money, goods or services in return for the promise of future payment."),
 ("p", "Credit can be useful: a shoe manufacturer who takes a loan for raw materials earns profit and can repay the loan; but credit can be a **debt trap** if the crop fails or income falls. A farmer who takes a loan and loses the crop may have to sell land to repay. Hence credit helps if the income from the loan is higher than the interest."),
 ("h2", "Terms of Credit"),
 ("table", ["Term", "Meaning"], [["Interest rate", "The amount to be paid on the principal"], ["Collateral", "An asset that the borrower owns (land, building, vehicle, livestock, deposits with banks) and uses as a guarantee until the loan is repaid; if the borrower fails to repay, the lender has the right to sell it"], ["Documentation requirements", "Papers to be submitted for the loan"], ["Mode of repayment", "Schedule and form of repaying the loan"]]),
 ("p", "Terms of credit vary across lenders and borrowers: **informal lenders** often charge a very high interest rate and take away produce or labour, while banks charge a fixed interest."),
 ("h2", "Formal Sector Credit in India"),
 ("table", ["", "Formal sector", "Informal sector"], [["Lenders", "Banks and cooperatives", "Moneylenders, traders, employers, relatives and friends"], ["Supervision", "**RBI** supervises: banks maintain minimum cash balance, give loans not only to profit-making businesses but also to small cultivators, small scale industries and small borrowers; they must report to the RBI on how much they lend, to whom and at what interest rate", "No organisation supervises; they can lend at any rate"], ["Interest", "Lower", "High; borrowers fall into debt-traps"], ["Terms", "Documents, collateral", "Easy, personal contact, but exploitative"]]),
 ("ul", ["Cheap and affordable credit is important for the country's development; there is a need to increase **formal sources of credit**, particularly for poor households; most loans of poor households come from informal sources, while rich households borrow mainly from the formal sector.", "Banks in the formal sector are not available in many rural areas and small borrowers lack collateral and papers; **cooperatives** also provide credit to farmers."]),
 ("h2", "Self-Help Groups for the Poor"),
 ("ul", ["Poor households, particularly women, find it difficult to get loans because they lack collateral. **Self-Help Groups (SHGs)** are small groups of 15-20 members, usually women of a neighbourhood, who pool their savings every month (Rs 25 to Rs 100 or more); loans are given within the group at a reasonable interest rate for purposes such as buying seeds or setting up small businesses; and after a period of regular savings, the group can borrow from the bank in its own name.", "Savings are used for members' needs; the group decides on the loan, interest and repayment; non-repayment is rare as the group monitors; **loans are small** and the interest goes to the group's savings. SHGs help to overcome the lack of collateral, and the **organisation** helps women to discuss social issues such as health, nutrition and domestic violence.", "**Grameen Bank of Bangladesh**, founded by **Muhammad Yunus** (Nobel Peace Prize 2006), is a large example of providing credit to the poor with no collateral; repayment rates are high."]),
 ("h2", "Quick Revision: Likely Exam Questions"),
 ("qa", [
  ("What is double coincidence of wants?", "Barter needs each party to want what the other offers."),
  ("Who issues currency in India?", "RBI, on behalf of the central government."),
  ("Why are demand deposits called money?", "They can be withdrawn on demand and used for payments through cheques."),
  ("What is collateral?", "An asset pledged as security for a loan."),
  ("Why is informal credit costly?", "High interest, no supervision, risk of debt traps."),
  ("What is an SHG?", "A group of 15-20 members who save together and lend to each other."),
  ("Who supervises formal lenders?", "The Reserve Bank of India."),
 ]),
 ],
 takeaways=["Money removes the **double coincidence of wants** of barter.", "Modern money is **currency and bank deposits**; RBI issues notes.", "Banks keep about **15 per cent** of deposits and lend the rest, earning from the interest gap.", "**Formal credit** is supervised by the RBI; **informal credit** is costly and exploitative.", "**Self-help groups** and the Grameen model give the poor credit without collateral."])
